Q: Round the time to either 30 or 60 minute mark (half-past current hour, or at the next hour), in Java A: <p><strong>Caveat:</strong> I am <em>not</em> certain this solution works for all time anomalies possible in political time.</p>
<h2><em>java.time</em></h2>
<p>In modern Java, use only the <em>java.time</em> classes.</p>
<p>To track a moment as seen through the wall-clock/calendar of a particular time zone, use <code>java.time.ZonedDateTime</code> class.</p>
<p>Be careful not to assume 60-minute hours, with minutes running 0-59. Political time is defined by the whims of politicians. So some hours do not exist, some days run 23 hours or 25 hours or 23.5 hours, or any other number decreed.</p>
<p>So, while I have <em>not</em> thought this through carefully, I suspect we should focus on truncating to the hour, either the next hour, or the current hour plus 30 minutes. The <code>ZonedDateTime</code> class will adjust as necessary if there is no even hour or half-hour on that date in that zone.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public static ZonedDateTime roundToHalfHour ( final ZonedDateTime zonedDateTime )
{
    final ZonedDateTime truncatedToHour = zonedDateTime.truncatedTo( ChronoUnit.HOURS );
    final ZonedDateTime halfPastTheHour = truncatedToHour.plusMinutes( 30 );
    if ( zonedDateTime.isAfter( halfPastTheHour ) )
    {
        return truncatedToHour.plusHours( 1 ); // Move to the next hour.
    }
    else
    {
        return halfPastTheHour;  // Move to half-past the hour.
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Example usage in Java 25:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>ZoneId zone = ZoneId.of( &quot;America/Montevideo&quot; );
ZonedDateTime zdt = ZonedDateTime.now( zone );
ZonedDateTime rounded = RoundingToTheHalfHour.roundToHalfHour( zdt );
</code></pre>
<p>When run:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>2026-01-26T18:58:24.525074-03:00[America/Montevideo]
2026-01-26T19:00-03:00[America/Montevideo]
</code></pre>

Q: Avalonia library DataGrid not showing rows A: <p>Make sure you add the StyleInclude in the App.xaml:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;Application.Styles&gt;
    &lt;FluentTheme /&gt;
    &lt;StyleInclude Source=&quot;avares://Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid/Themes/Fluent.xaml&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/Application.Styles&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>I missed that, and it took me a lot of time to find the reason why my rows didn't show up.</p>

Q: How to configure Spring for Async / Scheduling and WebSocket Messaging? A: <pre><code>Single pool cannot be shared for all. Each concern — @Async methods, @Scheduled tasks, and the WebSocket message broker — has different load patterns and operational requirements:

Resource Isolation: If one area (e.g., a long-running scheduled task) exhausts threads, it won't block async methods or WebSocket messaging.
Fine-Tuning: We can tune pool sizes and queue capacities based on usage and expected load for each subsystem.
Diagnostics: We can more easily track bottlenecks and resource starvation if pools are separated.
Spring’s own documentation and best practices recommend separating these pools to avoid thread starvation and performance problems.

Example: Unified Config Class for Async, Scheduling, and WebSocket Messaging
We can configure all three in a single @Configuration that also implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer:

Java
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableAsync;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.EnableScheduling;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskExecutor;
import org.springframework.scheduling.concurrent.ThreadPoolTaskScheduler;
import org.springframework.messaging.simp.config.MessageBrokerRegistry;
import org.springframework.web.socket.config.annotation.EnableWebSocketMessageBroker;
import org.springframework.web.socket.config.annotation.WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer;

import java.util.concurrent.Executor;

@Configuration
@EnableAsync
@EnableScheduling
@EnableWebSocketMessageBroker
public class AppConfig implements WebSocketMessageBrokerConfigurer {

    --Thread pool for @Async methods
    @Bean(name = &quot;asyncExecutor&quot;, destroyMethod = &quot;shutdown&quot;)
    public Executor asyncExecutor() {
        ThreadPoolTaskExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolTaskExecutor();
        executor.setCorePoolSize(10);
        executor.setMaxPoolSize(50);
        executor.setQueueCapacity(100);
        executor.setThreadNamePrefix(&quot;Async-&quot;);
        executor.initialize();
        return executor;
    }

    --Thread pool for @Scheduled tasks
    @Bean(name = &quot;taskScheduler&quot;)
    public ThreadPoolTaskScheduler taskScheduler() {
        ThreadPoolTaskScheduler scheduler = new ThreadPoolTaskScheduler();
        scheduler.setPoolSize(5);
        scheduler.setThreadNamePrefix(&quot;Scheduler-&quot;);
        scheduler.initialize();
        return scheduler;
    }

   --Dedicated scheduler for WebSocket Message Broker
    @Bean(name = &quot;brokerTaskScheduler&quot;)
    public ThreadPoolTaskScheduler brokerTaskScheduler() {
        ThreadPoolTaskScheduler scheduler = new ThreadPoolTaskScheduler();
        scheduler.setPoolSize(10);
        scheduler.setThreadNamePrefix(&quot;Broker-&quot;);
        scheduler.initialize();
        return scheduler;
    }

    @Override
    public void configureMessageBroker(MessageBrokerRegistry config) {
        config.enableSimpleBroker(&quot;/topic&quot;)
              .setTaskScheduler(brokerTaskScheduler()); // explicitly set
        config.setApplicationDestinationPrefixes(&quot;/app&quot;);
    }
}
Notes:
Must give unique bean names so each pool is used where appropriate.
Spring picks up the @Async executor by the bean name asyncExecutor.
Spring picks up the @Scheduled scheduler by the TaskScheduler bean ( named taskScheduler by default).
For WebSocket's simple broker, explicitly set your scheduler using setTaskScheduler(...).
Tune corePoolSize, maxPoolSize, and queue sizes based on workload and hardware.
</code></pre>

Q: AJAX receives empty string when the response is huge on MS edge\chrome A: <p>The default XHR response type is <code>text</code> which gets loaded into a JS string. I had this happen and noticed responses around 500MB would end up with empty data. I believe it may be due to a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/length#description" rel="nofollow noreferrer">string max size of 512MB in V8</a>.</p>
<p>What worked in my case was to change the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/XMLHttpRequest/responseType" rel="nofollow noreferrer">responseType</a> to either <code>blob</code> or <code>arraybuffer</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>$.ajax({
  xhrFields: {
    responseType: &quot;blob&quot;
  }
})
</code></pre>
